## 6. Decide the lifecycle-model gate

`factory/state-machine-research.md` proposes one shared typed transition table with exhaustive bounded checking. Its implementation is deferred until lifecycle qualification reveals whether it materially prevents invalid states.

- [ ] Compare qualification failures and recovery complexity with the states and transitions proposed in `factory/state-machine-research.md`.
- [ ] If the model prevents observed invalid states, implement one authoritative typed transition table shared by orchestration and tests.
- [ ] Exhaustively enumerate bounded global and per-issue states and reject illegal transitions.
- [ ] Cover capacity, one-worker ownership, attempt limits, changed heads, current-head review, CI, conflict resolution, merge, queue backpressure, automation claims, retries, and terminal blockers.
- [ ] If the table adds no material protection, record the rejection and identify the serialized and durable controls that make it unnecessary.
- [ ] Avoid a second lifecycle implementation that can drift from the plugin.

Completion criterion: either a compact executable model guards the lifecycle or a durable decision explains why existing controls are sufficient.

## 7. Apply the final seal

This is the final explicit-human-authority step. Perform it only after every preceding activation criterion passes.

- [ ] Re-run `bin/check-dev-env` at the exact proposed activation head.
- [ ] Confirm GitHub CI is green at that exact head.
- [ ] Obtain a final independent current-head review with no unresolved feedback.
- [ ] Verify the working tree is clean and the activation commit is present on `origin/main`.
- [ ] Enable branch protection on `main`.
- [ ] Require the repository's passing CI checks.
- [ ] Require owner-signed current heads for factory changes.
- [ ] Restrict ordinary direct pushes so non-chief work enters through issue workers, review, and merge gates.
- [ ] Seal the invariant that every role except chief of staff runs in an orb.
- [ ] Reverify main, twin-validation, and holdout-validation credential isolation after protection changes.
- [ ] Confirm publishing, destructive operations, account changes, and future activation changes still require explicit authority.
- [ ] Owner-sign the exact activation head.
- [ ] Issue the single explicit “go” that starts unattended execution.

Completion criterion: the signed and protected activation head is the reviewed head, all required checks are enforced, secret boundaries remain intact, and unattended execution cannot bypass the factory lifecycle.

## Activation blockers

Stop activation if any of these remains true:

- [ ] Scheduled work can be silently dropped, duplicated, or run without a timeout.
- [ ] A finding-producing automation can run while its output queue is full.
- [ ] One issue can acquire multiple workers.
- [ ] A stale review or stale CI result can authorize merge.
- [ ] Restart recovery depends on in-memory state alone.
- [ ] Failed or exhausted runs lack durable terminal status and usage accounting.
- [ ] A non-main automation route is configured without an executable isolated dispatcher.
- [ ] Any role can access credentials outside its project boundary.
- [ ] Any non-chief role can perform sealed work outside an orb.
- [ ] Branch protection or owner-signature enforcement points at a different head than the final reviewed head.

Activation is ready only when every applicable checklist item is complete and every blocker condition is false.
